    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The ever-increasing number of student enrollment in massive open online courses (MOOCs) is challenging the scalability of grading student submissions and giving precise feedback for student learning. Datasets of student submissions have started to be created, lighting the path for research into scalable machine learning algorithms to grade and provide feedback for student submissions. Wu et.al. developed a meta-learning method in giving feedback for student submissions in computer science. We frame the feedback challenge as a rubric grading problem, such that the selected rubrics for a submission has insightful feedback for the student. We explored the methodologies set forth by Wu et. al. on a broader set of student submissions using the Gradescope dataset, which is a collection of student submissions in subjects ranging from math, biology, chemistry, among others. The main contributions of this work is a thorough exploration of the performance of the meta-learning model on different subsets of the dataset and how different pre- trained models affect model performance. We have shown that the CodeBERT increases model performances on the overall rubric grading problem. Furthermore, we show that training on the Math data split results in the most robust model on out-of-domain distributions. We further illustrate rubric text design decisions that further increase model performance on the rubric grading problem.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Yatagai, T. (2024). Meta-Learning for Rubric Grading. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/rd528js6960. https://doi.org/10.25740/rd528js6960.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
